said. \"The other 8 billion conscious human beings on the planet have not signed up to this experiment and their moral rights, safety, and welfare are what we have an obligation to protect first and foremost.\"Conversations around whether AI models could one day become conscious are picking up steam in the tech world. Last April, Anthropic started a research program to investigate and prepare to navigate \"model welfare.\" In a blog post, the startup questioned whether developers should be concerned about models' potential consciousness and experiences.Google DeepMind published research papers this year about AI consciousness and how ascribing that characteristic could present challenges.On X, Suleyman said that if an AI model acts as if it suffers, or genuinely believes it can suffer, it will likely prioritize its own needs over operational objectives. As a result, they could harm humans because they're competing for resources, such as compute and energy, to pursue their own goals.\"We should only design AIs to so they are not conscious and not deserving of rights, and with the sole purpose to accelerate human wellbeing without being driven by their own intrinsic motivations.. We could then see whether this is easier or harder to align and keep safe,\" he said. Read next Business Insider tells the innovative stories you want to know Business Insider tells the innovative stories you want to know Business Insider tells the innovative stories you want to know Business Insider tells the innovative stories you want to know Lauren Edmonds You're currently following this author! Click to unsubscribe from email alerts. Microsoft AI","fetched_at":"2026-11-02T10:11:41.895Z","ok":true}